ling shift toward Pearson, could move this toward a genuine contest.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Steve Cohen (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Steve Cohen's retirement leaves this redrawn Tennessee 9th District open, and redistricting has shifted what was long a Democratic stronghold toward the Republicans. State Senator Brent Taylor won the GOP nomination and enters the general with a partisan lean and a favorable national environment on his side. State Representative Justin Pearson, a prominent progressive, has raised more money than Taylor and drawn endorsements from Kamala Harris and national groups.\n\nThe only public poll, from early September, shows Taylor narrowly ahead, and prediction markets give Pearson roughly a one-in-eight chance. Expert raters unanimously call the seat a Republican flip, though a few stop short of Solid R. The race leans clearly to Taylor, with Pearson's fundraising and profile the main sources of residual uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1065, "margin": -7.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.094, "margin": -9.0}]}